  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation.",
  "worked_solution": "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\omega t+\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $\u03d5$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,\u27f9\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(12t+\u03d5).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=0\\,\u27f9\\,\u03d5=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}\u206112t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=12R\\operatorname{cos}\u206112t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=12R\\,\u27f9\\,R=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u206112t.\n$$",
